  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species occurs in the western Pacific from the Ryukyu Islands&#160;of Japan to&#160;the Philippines, eastern Sulawesi (Indonesia), the Mariana Islands, south to the northern Great Barrier Reef of Australia, east to the Solomon Islands and New Caledonia to the Tuamotu Archipelago (Allen and Erdmann 2012, iDigBio database accessed March 2019). Its depth range is 1-365 metres (Allen and Erdmann 2012).",
    "population": "Population data are limited.",
    "habitats": "This species inhabits outer rocky and coral reef slopes and caves. Smaller individuals (under 12 cm) occur to 40 m depth and larger individuals to 365 m. The maximum length is 34 cm (Allen and Erdmann 2012). It feeds on planktonic prey in groups or pairs at night and retreats to caves and coral reefs during the day (Hellinger <em>et al.</em>&#160;2017).",
    "threats": "There are no known major threats.",
    "measures": "There are no species-specific conservation actions.",
    "use_trade": "Some species in the family Anomalopidae are used in public aquariums and are sometimes targeted by local fishers for bait (Paxton and Johnson 2002).",
    "rationale": "This widely distributed, reef-associated species has no known major threats; therefore, it is listed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
